WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.212 --> 00:00:06.600
ALEXA GRIFFITH: Hi, my
name is Alexa Griffith.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.895
Yes, my name really is
Alexa, but hopefully I

00:00:08.895 --> 00:00:10.410
won't echo today.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.500
And I'm presenting with
my amazing [INAUDIBLE],,

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:14.823
Mike Hurwitz, who
we call Danger.

00:00:14.823 --> 00:00:17.490
We're both Software Engineers on
the Data Science Infrastructure

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.290
Team at Bluecore,
and today, we're

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.720
going to talk about how we
use Google Cloud to serve

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.280
tens of thousands
of personalized

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.636
recommendations per second.

00:00:25.636 --> 00:00:28.870
At Bluecore we have a saying,
"As simple as possible,

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:30.620
as powerful as necessary."

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:32.119
This is one of our
company values,

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:34.390
and we will keep this
in mind as we discuss

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:36.610
the evolution of building
our robust recommendation

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:38.600
service throughout this talk.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.290
So GCP has enabled us
to build a service that

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:43.720
has this value of being
as simple as possible,

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:45.455
and as powerful as necessary.

00:00:45.455 --> 00:00:47.080
In building the
recommendation service,

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.080
we're able to leverage the
wide variety of GCP tools

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:53.163
to store, query, and serve data.

00:00:53.163 --> 00:00:54.580
They all [INAUDIBLE]
some purpose,

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:56.538
but really, it's the
breadth of their offerings

00:00:56.538 --> 00:00:58.960
that makes services like
ours possible, while allowing

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.470
us to maintain a low
operational overhead.

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:06.360
So you might be wondering,
what is Bluecore?

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.690
Bluecore offers intelligent
e-commerce marketing

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:10.010
as a service.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:12.710
We send you marketing
emails that don't suck,

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:15.840
and we aim to send you
better emails, not more.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.760
We're a company that's
around 7 years old.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.700
We have around 250 employees.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.578
We serve hundreds
of large retailers,

00:01:21.578 --> 00:01:23.120
including some you
may have heard of,

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.340
like Nike and Sephora.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:28.310
What makes us different is that
we don't send the same email

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:29.660
to every person.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:33.140
Almost all of our millions of
emails that we send every day

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:34.868
are personalized.

00:01:34.868 --> 00:01:36.410
Additionally, we've
started providing

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:38.965
real-time recommendations
on retailers' websites.

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:43.190
And we're going to refer to
this as our on-site product.

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:44.600
So let's discuss.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.265
What even is a recommendation?

00:01:46.265 --> 00:01:48.560
Well, when we talk
about a recommendation,

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.060
we're talking about
products that we

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:52.850
think someone will like based
on either a product they've

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:55.580
interacted with, or they
may have a preference for,

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:59.020
given their purchase and
viewing history in the past.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:02.020
Essentially, we map either
an email or a product

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:05.480
ID to a list of
recommended products.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:07.580
For example, in these
cases, two recipients

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:10.820
of the same campaign, which is
just the type of email send,

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:12.750
left different items
in their carts.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:15.380
And we call these
"abandoned cart emails."

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:17.760
If we take a look at
the email on the left,

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.567
the person's left
jeans in their cart.

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:21.650
It's possible that, based
on their past purchasing

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.510
and viewing history,
we want to recommend

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:26.270
to them a cami or a t-shirt.

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:28.490
On the other hand,
in the right email,

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:31.310
a person has left a collared
shirt in their cart.

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:34.190
We may recommend other products
similar to the collared shirt

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.350
as products the
user may also like.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:40.020
For example, here we recommend
other types of collared shirts.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.020
But there are some
things to consider.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:44.570
It's not just as easy as putting
some pictures in an email

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:45.690
and sending the email.

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:47.940
There are certain things we
need to take into account,

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:52.140
like product exclusion rules,
which are evaluated at runtime.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.130
What a product's exclusion rule
could look like is you say,

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:57.380
in the email, you don't want
to show a product that's

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:58.932
on discount or out of stock.

00:02:58.932 --> 00:03:00.890
Or maybe even you want
to show a product that's

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:02.900
recently come back into stock.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:04.490
Since products
update constantly,

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:06.950
it's very important that
we have an accurate catalog

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:10.150
and don't show invalid
products in the email.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:12.120
This requirement definitely
makes our service

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:13.360
a bit more interesting.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.480
And we'll talk about how
we use our Product Update

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.100
Listener to keep our
product catalog fresh.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:20.760
MARK HURWITZ: So a marketer's
decided to send an email,

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.140
and they create a campaign.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:24.990
The campaign has an
audience and a template.

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:28.697
A template has blocks, which may
contain recommended products.

00:03:28.697 --> 00:03:30.780
The template is mixed with
the audience query that

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.610
runs in BigQuery,
and with those, we

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:34.560
can generate the emails.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:37.050
Products are then joined with
the audience in the query,

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.930
which means that any decisions
the template makes cannot rely

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:42.960
on anything that the
query is unaware of.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.270
Generating audiences this
way is very flexible,

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:47.820
and we consider it to be
a competitive advantage.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:52.460
But applying recommendations
at this stage is very limiting.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:55.632
So you may be asking,
is this good enough?

00:03:55.632 --> 00:03:57.880
On-site was a new
frontier for Bluecore

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:00.460
that forced us to
re-evaluate our architecture.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:05.030
Latency became a primary
driver, not just bandwidth.

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:06.718
Audience generation
and personalization

00:04:06.718 --> 00:04:08.510
are separate, which
means the templates are

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:12.440
limited to data appended to
the rows of the audience query.

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:14.960
We want to support templates
that are structurally dynamic,

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:16.959
rather than just
filling in blocks.

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:19.459
And that means either making
the audience query aware

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:21.990
of all of the decisions
that need to be made,

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:24.620
which is pretty difficult,
or allowing the template

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:26.510
to pull the
recommendations it knows

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:29.710
it needs at runtime,
on a per-email basis.

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:32.750
A/B testing is another example
of a personalized runtime

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:35.510
decision that's difficult
to make in the database.

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.570
Previously, we were copying some
recommendations into Datastore

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:40.390
to support these use cases.

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:43.160
And while Datastore is
great, this is really not

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:44.140
what it was made for.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:46.110
And it got to be very expensive.

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:49.110
So we had to build
something new.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:50.970
Pages on the web
start to feel slow

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:55.050
at 300 to 400 milliseconds,
and recommendations is only

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:57.438
part of the on-site
campaign process.

00:04:57.438 --> 00:04:58.980
There's also generating
the template,

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:02.190
and translating all that
data to and from the browser.

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:04.260
So we gave ourselves
about 100 milliseconds

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:06.000
to do all the work
that we needed to do

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.160
on the recommendation side.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:09.810
We want to be able
to serve about 50

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:11.705
million emails an hour.

00:05:11.705 --> 00:05:13.080
And if you do a
little math, that

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:15.900
works out to 13,000
emails a second.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:17.850
We targeted 20,000
emails a second,

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:20.190
because we figured that
would be enough headroom.

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:22.560
Doing product
filtering in the email

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.620
means that there must be
enough products returned

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:28.470
that the filters will be
run across all of them,

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.940
and there'll still be enough
to fill in the blocks.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:33.930
That means sending more products
than are actually required down

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:36.150
into the templating engine.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:37.890
We wanted to move
those decisions up

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:40.200
into the recommendation
service, so that we were only

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.240
returning the products
that we needed,

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.920
rather than returning too many.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:46.680
Or potentially,
even worse, too few.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.860
Supporting recommendations
via A/B tests

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:52.120
is vital, because
frankly, without testing

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:53.260
you can't improve.

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:55.200
And we want to do all
of this without spending

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.306
all of our money.

00:05:57.306 --> 00:05:59.450
ALEXA GRIFFITH: So for
the naive implementation,

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:01.530
we want to change as
little as possible.

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:04.190
We know this probably won't
be good enough in the end,

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:06.920
but what it will do is it will
give us a good idea of what

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:08.940
areas we need to improve on.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:11.370
So if we want to change
as little as possible,

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.320
how would we do it?

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:16.250
Well, we would choose to write
a GRPC service to communicate

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:17.812
with our templating
engine, and we

00:06:17.812 --> 00:06:19.520
want to go ahead and
make use of the data

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:21.130
that we already have available.

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:23.630
Our recommendations data
already listed in BigQuery,

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:26.820
along with our products data
that comes from Datastore.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:29.570
So this would be the minimum
we need for our service

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:32.790
to do runtime personalization.

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:34.390
The recommendation
service makes up

00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:36.660
one of three parts
of personalization,

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:38.410
with the other two
being templating, which

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:41.170
comes from Datastore, and
the audience, which is just

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:43.060
all the people that
receive an email

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:44.510
and is stored in BigQuery.

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:47.410
The recommendation service
runs in Kubernetes' engine,

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:48.520
and is written in Go.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:50.590
and the products data
lives in Datastore,

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:53.110
while the recommendations live
mainly in BigQuery, with some

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:55.150
copied into Datastore.

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:57.720
So now we've checked the
"as simple as possible" box.

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:01.698
But do we meet the "as powerful
as necessary" requirements?

00:07:01.698 --> 00:07:03.490
Well, when considering
if our service meets

00:07:03.490 --> 00:07:06.490
these requirements, we're going
to evaluate the recommendations

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:08.300
and products components.

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:10.270
Remember, we now have
to consider on-site,

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:12.850
or the recommendations
on the company's website,

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:14.800
as part of the requirements.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:18.460
BigQuery is great for bandwidth,
but now latency is our problem.

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:21.640
We're failing to meet our
on-site latency requirements.

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:23.980
Even if we did export all
of our recommendations

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:26.230
from BigQuery to
Datastore, we still

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:28.180
might not hit our latency goals.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:32.410
And keep in mind, for us, using
Datastore is expensive here.

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:35.140
But we can't blame a hammer
for being a bad screwdriver.

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:36.588
We just need the right tools.

00:07:36.588 --> 00:07:38.380
So first, we're going
to discuss how we can

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:41.170
liberate our recommendations.

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:43.450
As I just mentioned,
BigQuery cannot be the source

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:45.110
of recommendations at runtime.

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:46.910
The latency is just too high.

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:50.270
So we looked into
Bigtable instead.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:52.110
Why Bigtable, you may be asking.

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:54.200
Well, caching is not
a good option for us

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:57.170
because the specific
recommendation data for a given

00:07:57.170 --> 00:08:00.525
recommendation type,
email, and namespace,

00:08:00.525 --> 00:08:02.150
are often only read
once before they're

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:03.710
invalidated in the cache.

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:05.690
And a cache miss would
mean reading directly

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:08.330
from BigQuery, which
costs us in latency,

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:10.880
or Datastore,
which is expensive.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:13.050
On the other hand,
Bigtable latencies

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:14.990
are around 15 to
20 milliseconds,

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:16.880
and better the
harder you hit it.

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:19.550
So if we consolidate
our data into Bigtable,

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:22.660
we can meet our
latency's requirements.

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:24.820
Our recommendations,
including events,

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:26.860
and our generated
recommendations from our data

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:29.470
science models, are
still stored in BigQuery.

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:31.120
Additionally, we
started generating

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:33.530
some of our recommendations
using data proc,

00:08:33.530 --> 00:08:35.559
and these are stored in GCS.

00:08:35.559 --> 00:08:37.039
We created a service
written in Go

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:39.340
that's a Kubernetes
task running in Airflow,

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:42.010
just to migrate and
consolidate our recommendations

00:08:42.010 --> 00:08:44.080
data into Bigtable.

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:47.950
The GCS and BigQuery storage
API made loading data

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:50.050
into Bigtable fairly easy.

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:53.350
With a small Bigtable
cluster of just three nodes,

00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:55.290
we're able to load,
read, and write

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:58.910
150,000 to 200,000
rows per second.

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:00.898
So now that we have
our data into Bigtable,

00:09:00.898 --> 00:09:03.190
let's talk a little bit about
how we deal with our data

00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:04.630
in Bigtable.

00:09:04.630 --> 00:09:07.630
We have hundreds of namespaces,
but Bigtable has a 1,000-table

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:09.250
quota.

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:11.568
We can't have a
per-namespace table for each

00:09:11.568 --> 00:09:14.110
of our recommendation models,
or we're going to quickly reach

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:16.210
that 1,000-table quota limit.

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:17.140
That's OK, though.

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:19.310
They don't call it
"Mediumtable" for a reason.

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:21.530
This is what Bigtable
was made for.

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:23.650
So instead, we
have a per-table--

00:09:23.650 --> 00:09:26.110
we have a
table-per-recommendation model,

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:28.840
and our schema is the key
as the partner and ID,

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:30.490
which ID could be email.

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:33.380
And the value is a
list of product ID's.

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:36.460
So just to recap, this
is what we had before,

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:38.380
paying attention to the
recommendations coming

00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:41.050
from BigQuery and
Datastore, which are now

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:45.730
consolidated into Bigtable
via our BigQuery-to-Bigtable

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:46.760
service.

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:49.700
And we picked up literally
seconds of latency.

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:51.700
So now that we've improved
the recommendation

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:54.070
component of the
service, are we good now?

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:56.760
Are we meeting our on-site
latency requirements?

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:59.800
Well, I mentioned previously,
on the previous slide,

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:02.170
that there are two areas
where we can improve

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:03.720
our recommendation service.

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:06.370
The recommendations
portion and the products.

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:08.810
Now that we've improved
recommendations,

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:11.350
we're still constantly fetching
products and Datastore,

00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:13.480
causing it to burn a
hole in our wallets.

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:16.180
Additionally, we're failing the
on-site latency requirements

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:18.190
because of this too.

00:10:18.190 --> 00:10:21.280
So we measured Datastore latency
from a GKE application written

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:22.960
in Python, and each
of the products

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:26.775
we fetched from Datastore are
around 2 kilobytes per entity.

00:10:26.775 --> 00:10:28.900
We found that the Datastore
latencies aren't great.

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:31.190
They're about 45
milliseconds to get started,

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:34.480
and then an additional
4.2 milliseconds per key.

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:35.623
So like I said, not great.

00:10:35.623 --> 00:10:37.165
But they aren't
totally disqualifying

00:10:37.165 --> 00:10:38.630
for our use of Datastore.

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:40.540
MARK HURWITZ: Using
Datastore didn't leave us

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:41.930
a lot of headroom.

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:44.470
So if Datastore isn't going
to get us where we need to go,

00:10:44.470 --> 00:10:46.510
we need to find something else.

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:51.390
What we need is a
latency smack-down!

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:53.830
Our objects are
about 2 kilobytes,

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:56.540
and so these are cachable.

00:10:56.540 --> 00:10:59.150
We found Memorystore to be
about two orders of magnitude

00:10:59.150 --> 00:11:00.550
faster than Datastore.

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:02.240
Fetching a single
key out of Datastore

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:04.580
cost about 50 milliseconds.

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:06.230
But Memorystore,
for the same load,

00:11:06.230 --> 00:11:10.200
costs only 125 microseconds.

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:13.360
We're usually reading about
20 to 40 products per request,

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:16.780
so these numbers are more
realistic than you might guess.

00:11:16.780 --> 00:11:18.390
Because Memorystore
is managed Redis,

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:20.410
it was easy to find
clients that support

00:11:20.410 --> 00:11:22.480
all the features that
we need for performance,

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:23.890
such as pipelining.

00:11:23.890 --> 00:11:25.610
We need to avoid the
calls to Datastore,

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:28.120
which means we need to have
a long TTL on our cache.

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:30.520
But Alexa already told us
the products are constantly

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:31.143
changing.

00:11:31.143 --> 00:11:32.560
They're going in
and out of stock.

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:34.970
Prices are changing,
images are changing.

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:37.120
So we can't have a long TTL.

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:39.640
Our product can just
pipeline right to Datastore,

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:41.080
as it always has.

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:44.020
But now, it's also
notifying us of invalidation

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:47.500
by pushing product ID's
onto Google Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:50.020
We have a listener that reads
those ideas from Pub/Sub,

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:51.790
pulls the products
from Datastore,

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:54.040
and writes them to our cache.

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:56.740
You may be wondering, why do
we send ID's instead of just

00:11:56.740 --> 00:11:58.630
sending the product bodies?

00:11:58.630 --> 00:12:00.910
Well, the product
[INAUDIBLE] is concurrent,

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:04.330
so we can't know of any single
update as authoritative.

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:06.880
By sending ID's instead
of bodies on Pub/Sub,

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:09.940
we can guarantee that the
cache is eventually consistent,

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:12.850
rather than being potentially
inconsistent forever.

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:14.860
And we can have a very long TTL.

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:16.840
Datastore natively
returns protobufs,

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:19.700
and those come out to about
2 kilobytes, on average.

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:23.860
Now, we could have come up with
a new serialization format that

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:26.350
does everything that
Datastore's native serialization

00:12:26.350 --> 00:12:29.410
format does, but there was
really no reason to do that,

00:12:29.410 --> 00:12:31.510
because it's already doing it.

00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:34.000
But with about 2
kilobytes per product,

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:36.070
and many, many
products, we'd end up

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:39.230
with a huge cache, which
is both expensive and slow.

00:12:39.230 --> 00:12:41.140
So, how do we mitigate that?

00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:43.510
The first thing is that we
don't update products that

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:45.400
aren't already in the cache.

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:46.990
Products get into
the cache by being

00:12:46.990 --> 00:12:49.750
used in a marketing
product, which

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:52.180
means that the only objects
that'll be in the cache

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:55.390
are the objects that are
actually being sent out.

00:12:55.390 --> 00:12:57.250
So if a partner of
ours happens to have

00:12:57.250 --> 00:12:59.890
a catalog of a million products,
but they're only sending out

00:12:59.890 --> 00:13:02.110
emails with 1,000
of those products,

00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:05.190
we only have to have
1,000 in our cache.

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:07.770
The second thing we looked
into was compression,

00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:10.110
but compressing
small items is hard.

00:13:10.110 --> 00:13:13.240
Most compression algorithms
learn as they go,

00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:15.300
which means if you're
compressing a book,

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:17.730
the first page is going to
compress significantly worse

00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:19.990
than the 10th page
or the 100th page.

00:13:19.990 --> 00:13:23.190
And with only 2 kilobytes,
there really isn't a lot of time

00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:23.925
to learn.

00:13:23.925 --> 00:13:25.800
By the time you get to
the end of the product

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:27.840
and finally know
how to compress it,

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:29.880
there's no more to compress.

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:31.000
So what did we do?

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:34.920
Well, first thing is that
we downloaded 1,858 products

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:37.270
from Datastore for
one of our partners,

00:13:37.270 --> 00:13:39.450
which came out to
about 32 megabytes.

00:13:39.450 --> 00:13:42.900
And we then tried to compress
them using gzip and xz.

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:46.800
And while the LZMA
compression of xz

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:49.710
is really too slow
for runtime, that's

00:13:49.710 --> 00:13:55.410
kind of OK, because we're
really just testing here.

00:13:55.410 --> 00:14:00.120
So we then found that we
were unable to do better

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:03.710
than 2-to-1 compression,
even with the very slow xz.

00:14:03.710 --> 00:14:05.980
Well, that's not
really good enough.

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:07.020
So how good could it be?

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:09.970
Well, we took those
the same 1,858 products

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:12.630
and we tarred them all
together, effectively making one

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:14.870
big book, and compressed again.

00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:17.850
And we found that gzip was
able to do better than 5-to-1,

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:20.970
and xz was even able to
do better than 8-to-1.

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:23.490
But we can't just tar
these products at runtime.

00:14:23.490 --> 00:14:25.833
That doesn't make
any sense, because we

00:14:25.833 --> 00:14:27.000
don't need all the products.

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:28.980
We don't know
which ones we need.

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:31.140
So we needed something else.

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:34.290
Fortunately for us,
Zstandard exists,

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:37.663
which allows you to train its
compression ahead of time.

00:14:37.663 --> 00:14:39.330
They call this
"dictionary compression,"

00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:41.160
and that's exactly what we do.

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:42.840
On a per-partner
basis, we download

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:45.000
a sample of the
products, compress them,

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:46.380
training the dictionary.

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:49.260
And then we store those
dictionaries into Datastore,

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:51.520
caching them in the
service as well.

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:53.640
So any time we read
or write a product,

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:55.710
we know exactly what
dictionary to use.

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:57.660
And the compression
is fantastic.

00:14:57.660 --> 00:14:59.880
As you can see here,
Zstandard with dictionary

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:04.320
is better than 5-to-1, even on
small, 2-kilobyte [INAUDIBLE]..

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:07.530
There are other high-performance
and decompression algorithms,

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:10.090
such as Snappy and LZ4.

00:15:10.090 --> 00:15:12.810
However, those don't offer
dictionary compression, which

00:15:12.810 --> 00:15:16.500
as we've seen, is a big
factor with such small bodies.

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:18.970
As a reminder, this is
where we were before,

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:20.670
and this is where we are now.

00:15:20.670 --> 00:15:23.860
We have products still stored
in Datastore, as we always have.

00:15:23.860 --> 00:15:25.680
But now they're
being kept up-to-date

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:28.780
in a cache with our
Product Update Listener,

00:15:28.780 --> 00:15:31.370
which is receiving invalidation
messages over Google Cloud

00:15:31.370 --> 00:15:32.610
Pub/Sub.

00:15:32.610 --> 00:15:35.160
The recommendation service
itself has a small LRU

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:38.340
cache with a short TTL, just
to keep the traffic-to-memory

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:40.440
stored down to a dull roar.

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:43.890
And we think we're finally
as powerful as necessary.

00:15:43.890 --> 00:15:46.290
But, how to be sure?

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:47.750
The only way is to test it.

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:50.550
And fortunately for
us, the power of GKE,

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:52.950
this was pretty straightforward.

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:54.435
We were able to
deploy our service,

00:15:54.435 --> 00:15:58.070
and to the attacking client,
into Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:15:58.070 --> 00:16:01.320
And we could then scale both
the service and that client

00:16:01.320 --> 00:16:02.890
with a single command.

00:16:02.890 --> 00:16:05.520
The entire run, including
re-scaling the components,

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:07.830
was completely
scripted and hands-off.

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:08.920
And how did we do?

00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:11.190
Well, we did pretty well.

00:16:11.190 --> 00:16:13.950
With 16 two-core pods
running the service,

00:16:13.950 --> 00:16:18.300
we were able to pull between 56
and 60 kilohertz, while still

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:20.490
staying within our
latency budget.

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:24.540
And we hit our target of 20
kilohertz with just four pods.

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:27.703
Yes, we are finally as
powerful as necessary.

00:16:27.703 --> 00:16:29.370
Now that this code
is out in production,

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:33.550
well, changes need to be made,
and we need to live with it.

00:16:33.550 --> 00:16:35.460
One thing that we do a lot is--

00:16:35.460 --> 00:16:37.470
when changes are made,
we'll deploy a canary

00:16:37.470 --> 00:16:38.580
into Kubernetes.

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:41.130
That allows us to validate
that the new code doesn't

00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:44.520
have a regression, in terms
of correctness or performance.

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:47.130
And then once that's
proven, we can scale it up

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:49.200
to the rest of our pods.

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:52.530
Logs are exported automatically
from GKE into the BigQuery,

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:55.230
and so we've got a one-stop
shop for when we need

00:16:55.230 --> 00:16:56.850
to look and see what happened.

00:16:56.850 --> 00:16:59.460
And we're exporting all
of our metrics to Datadog.

00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:01.760
And because this is
a production service,

00:17:01.760 --> 00:17:04.260
we've got PagerDuty to wake us
up in the middle of the night

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:06.339
if something catastrophic
should happen.

00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:07.450
So, what have we got?

00:17:07.450 --> 00:17:09.180
Well, recommendations
are being written

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:11.130
to BigQuery and
Google Cloud Storage,

00:17:11.130 --> 00:17:12.790
as they always have been.

00:17:12.790 --> 00:17:16.290
But now they're being copied
into Google Cloud Bigtable.

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:18.450
Products are stored
in Datastore, again,

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:21.960
as they always were, but are
now being cached in Memorystore

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:25.170
and kept up-to-date using
invalidation triggered

00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:27.359
through Google Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:17:27.359 --> 00:17:31.530
Our clients hit us using
either protobuf over HTTP/1.1,

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:34.050
or gRPC over HTTP2.

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:37.460
And the whole thing is deployed
into Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:39.512
I'd like to thank you
all for listening,

00:17:39.512 --> 00:17:41.720
and thank you to Google for
giving us the opportunity

00:17:41.720 --> 00:17:43.840
to speak to you today.

